  "texts": [
    "On Mount Everest, water boils at just 70 degrees.",
    "Why? Boiling depends on air pressure, not just heat.",
    "Above you sits a huge, heavy column of air.",
    "At sea level it presses with about 1013 hectopascals.",
    "Heating makes water molecules move faster and faster.",
    "The fastest ones form bubbles of steam inside the water.",
    "But the air pushes back and crushes the bubbles.",
    "Water boils once steam pushes as hard as the air.",
    "At sea level, that happens at 100 degrees.",
    "The higher you go, the less air is above you.",
    "On Everest, the pressure drops to about a third.",
    "Steam wins much sooner, so water boils at 70 degrees.",
    "That water is cooler, so eggs and pasta cook slower.",
    "A pressure cooker does the opposite and raises the pressure.",
    "Water inside boils around 120 degrees, so food cooks faster.",
    "Follow for more physics hiding in your kitchen!"
  ]
